[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 395,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 228,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58422,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 36,
        "headingId": 228,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Gardner fibroma is a benign plaque-like mass composed of thick, haphazardly arranged collagen bundles with sparsely interspersed fibroblasts, and it is usually associated with familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP).</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:22:15"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 396,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 229,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58423,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 36,
        "headingId": 229,
        "headingText": "<p>8810/0 Gardner fibroma</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-01-31T09:25:46"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 397,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 230,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58424,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 36,
        "headingId": 230,
        "headingText": "<p>EE6Y &amp; XH7GT0 Other specified fibromatous disorders of skin and soft tissue &amp; Gardner fibroma</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-01-23T16:19:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 398,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 231,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58425,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 36,
        "headingId": 231,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> Gardner-associated fibroma.</p>\r\n<p><em>Not recommended:</em> desmoid precursor lesion.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:22:15"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 399,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 232,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58426,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 36,
        "headingId": 232,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:22:15"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 400,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 233,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58427,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 36,
        "headingId": 233,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Gardner fibromas commonly arise in the superficial and deep soft tissues of the torso (usually the back or paraspinal region) or head/neck region, but almost any non-visceral anatomical site can be involved, including the mesentery {11342777; 17325483}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:22:15"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 401,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 234,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58428,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 36,
        "headingId": 234,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These tumours typically present as painless masses {11342777; 17325483}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:22:15"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 402,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 235,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58429,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 36,
        "headingId": 235,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Gardner fibroma is diagnosed most often in children aged &lt;&nbsp;10&nbsp;years but may present in young adults as well. There is no sex predilection {11342777; 17325483}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:22:15"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 403,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 236,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58430,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 36,
        "headingId": 236,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Approximately 80% of cases are associated with FAP, <em>APC</em> mutation, or familial desmoid fibromatosis {11342777; 17325483}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:22:15"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 404,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 237,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58431,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 36,
        "headingId": 237,
        "headingText": "<p>Most cases arise in patients with germline <em>APC</em> mutations (see <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/chaptercontent/33/40\" target=\"_blank\">section </a><em>Desmoid fibromatosis</em>).</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-02-19T15:45:48"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 411,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 244,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58432,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 36,
        "headingId": 244,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Gardner fibroma is a poorly circumscribed, rubbery, tan-white mass ranging in size from &lt;&nbsp;1&nbsp;cm to &gt;&nbsp;10&nbsp;cm {11342777; 17325483}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:22:15"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 405,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 238,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58433,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 36,
        "headingId": 238,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The dominant histopathological feature is the haphazard arrangement of coarse collagen fibres with characteristic clefts or cracks and few inconspicuous spindle cells {11342777; 17325483}. A helpful diagnostic feature is entrapment of adjacent adipose tissue and neurovascular bundles at the edge of the lesion. By immunohistochemistry, CD34 is almost always positive, whereas SMA is negative {11342777; 17325483}. Nuclear accumulation of &beta;-catenin is often observed, particularly in cases associated with FAP {17325483; 26840078}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:22:15"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 406,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 239,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58434,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 36,
        "headingId": 239,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:22:15"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 407,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 240,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58435,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 36,
        "headingId": 240,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:22:15"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 410,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 243,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58436,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 36,
        "headingId": 243,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> plaque-like mass in the paraspinal region or trunk of young children; haphazard arrangement of coarse collagen fibres with clefts or cracks and few inconspicuous spindle cells; expression of CD34 and nuclear &beta;-catenin.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:22:15"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 408,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 241,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58437,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 36,
        "headingId": 241,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:22:15"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 409,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 242,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 58438,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 36,
        "headingId": 242,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Gardner fibroma is benign but is associated with concurrent or subsequent development of desmoid fibromatosis in approximately 20% of cases {17325483; 9692575}. Conversely, an associated Gardner fibroma or an area in the desmoid tumour resembling Gardner fibroma can be identified in as many as 37% of patients with desmoid fibromatosis {25064609; 27124915}. Given the close association between Gardner fibroma and FAP, a diagnosis of Gardner fibroma should prompt screening of the family for FAP.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:22:15"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]